### 1. What the Commands Do

`grep`\
The `grep` command searches for specific words, patterns, or text in files. For example, `grep "ERROR" sys_events.log` displays all log entries containing “ERROR”.

`chown`\
The `chown` command changes the ownership of a file or directory. For example, `sudo chown user01 confidential.txt` changes the file’s owner to `user01`.

`usermod`\
The `usermod` command modifies an existing Linux user account. It can be used to change group membership, a login shell, or other account settings.

### 2. What I Learned from Accessing `confidential.txt`

I learned that changing a file’s ownership can affect who is able to access it, depending on the file’s permissions. Even after ownership changes, access is not automatically guaranteed; the user must have the appropriate read, write, or execute permissions. This demonstrated the importance of Linux file ownership and permissions in protecting confidential information.


